{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially beyond ~10–15 days. Apply a negative level shift (downward correction) that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: after a run-up, the model keeps projecting elevated/flat prices while the market mean-reverts lower. Add a regime rule: if last 5–10 days show a sharp rise, force a downward drift in the 2–4 week horizon.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when spot jumps into the low-70s, the model extrapolates further gains (often into 72–74) but outcomes peak sooner and then fall. Cap upside projections following spikes and increase probability/size of pullbacks.",
    "Underestimates near-term continuation during early rally phase: in the first ~1–7 days after a breakout, forecasts are slightly below realized (misses immediate momentum). Add a small short-horizon upward adjustment when recent returns are strongly positive.",
    "Horizon-dependent error shape: near-term errors are smaller and sometimes negative (under-forecast), while long-horizon errors are large and positive (over-forecast). Use a piecewise correction: slight +adj for days 1–7 in strong up-momentum regimes; progressively larger −adj for days 10–30, especially after a rally.",
    "Mean-reversion strength under-modeled: realized paths revert toward mid/high-60s faster than forecast implies. Increase mean-reversion speed parameter (or pull forecasts toward a rolling 20–30d mean) when price is >~2–3 EUR above that mean."
  ]
}
